    bottom. This is deliberate — it makes "what's due" readable at a glance, which is the same signal notifications will
    surface.
  - [1mDue dates[0m render relative ("in 2 days", "overdue by 1 day") with the absolute date in a [38;5;153mtitle[39m attribute. Overdue
    items get a visual marker.
  - [1mEditing:[0m click the title to edit inline; due date via a [38;5;153m<input type="date">[39m. Delete is a per-row button with no
    confirmation dialog — undo is out of scope (YAGNI).
  - [1mEmpty state:[0m a short message rather than a bare page. No search, no filters — not yet justified.
  - [1mAccessibility:[0m real [38;5;153m<form>[39m/[38;5;153m<label>[39m/[38;5;153m<input type="checkbox">[39m elements, the list as a [38;5;153m<ul>[39m, and a live region for
    validation and storage errors.

  [1mError handling[0m, the four cases worth naming: empty or whitespace-only title is rejected inline; an unparseable due
  date is rejected without clearing the form; a localStorage write failure (quota, private mode) shows a warning and
  keeps working from memory rather than losing the task silently; a corrupt read boots to an empty list with a banner
  instead of a white screen.

  Last section is [1mtooling[0m, and it's cheapest to settle before any code exists. Given the zero-dependency choice, my
  recommendation is Node's built-in test runner ([38;5;153mnode --test[39m) — it handles ES modules natively and adds nothing to
  [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m, and [38;5;153mstore.js[39m/[38;5;153mstorage.js[39m are pure logic so they test headlessly. E2E via Playwright is the one option
  that would meaningfully contradict the no-dependencies stance.

  If anything in the behavior section above looks wrong, say so and I'll revise before writing the spec.
